WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.009
Netflix, or that thing 30 percent of the people
watching this right now have open in another

00:00:05.009 --> 00:00:09.750
tab, is the world's most popular streaming
service with some 70 million plus subscribers.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:13.620
For anyone who's ever been a little curious
about that benevolent red logo that contains

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:18.200
so many streaming options, here are few little-known
facts about Netflix.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.040
The mythical origin story

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:24.210
You might've heard the story about how Netflix
founder Reed Hastings got the idea for the

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:28.510
company after being slapped with a $40 late
fee from Blockbuster after returning his rental

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:30.840
of Apollo 13 past its due date.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:34.890
That revenge narrative might be nice and all,
but the real story is a lot more complicated.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:39.030
Hastings basically made the tale up as a buzzy
beat for crowds, but the truth was that he

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:43.350
co-founded the site with the help of Marc
Randolph through a long and complicated process

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.270
that would've been a lot less headline-friendly.

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:49.180
As Randolph remembered, they first got the
idea for mail-in orders from Amazon and tested

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:54.149
out DVD durability by mailing one another
a CD and seeing if it could survive the transit.

00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:58.800
When it did, they got busy creating the catalogue
queue and no-late-fee model and started counting

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.840
up their subscribers.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.489
Bye bye Blockbuster

00:01:02.489 --> 00:01:06.530
When Netflix first began, it was a mail-order
service for movies and TV titles that offered

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:09.880
a convenience factor above the local brick
and mortar Blockbusters -- where you'd have

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:13.869
to grapple with finding a disc on its shelves
and face hefty late fees if you turned it

00:01:13.869 --> 00:01:14.869
in too late.

00:01:14.869 --> 00:01:18.159
But Netflix wasn't initially aiming to make
its central competitor obsolete; quite the

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:19.159
opposite.

00:01:19.159 --> 00:01:23.670
In 2000, the company offered Blockbuster the
chance to buy it out for $50 million and get

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.890
in on the digital streaming game during its
inception.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:29.709
That might seem like a deal now, but at the
time, it was a scoffable offense to the former

00:01:29.709 --> 00:01:31.130
powerhouse chain.

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:35.420
When the offer was made, Netflix was actually
hemorrhaging money which meant that the valuation

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:38.939
of $50 million was way more than the company
was actually worth.

00:01:38.939 --> 00:01:42.719
Also, Netflix didn't just want to be absorbed
by Blockbuster; they wanted to partner up

00:01:42.719 --> 00:01:45.319
to front the company's entire online presence.

00:01:45.319 --> 00:01:49.939
So, basically, it was a situation where a
feisty up-and-comer company went to its goliath

00:01:49.939 --> 00:01:52.329
rival and demanded money and power.

00:01:52.329 --> 00:01:57.310
"Um, here's a suggestion; um, have the money
by tomorrow and there won't be any problems."

00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:58.909
You can see why Blockbuster declined.

00:01:58.909 --> 00:02:02.810
Of course, in retrospect, it was the first
of many colossal mistakes that would bring

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:07.349
Blockbuster down and earn some serious schadenfreude
from all the mom-and-pop video shops that

00:02:07.349 --> 00:02:09.250
were destroyed during its own rise.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:11.819
But that's another story altogether.

00:02:11.819 --> 00:02:13.230
Adjusting the algorithm

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:17.670
Although Netflix got its start in postal pictures,
it's now been dwarfed by its own digital game.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:21.989
And while several competitors have since popped
up, including Hulu and Amazon Prime, Netflix

00:02:21.989 --> 00:02:26.020
is still a mogul in the marketplace, thanks
in large part to its unique approach to algorithms.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:30.930
While some services might simply classify
a movie or show based on their MPAA ratings

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:34.819
or genres, Netflix takes great lengths to
catalogue the titles in its library with more

00:02:34.819 --> 00:02:39.049
specific tags, like what era the movies take
place in, whether they were critically acclaimed,

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:41.400
and if they belong to a niche sub genre.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.000
"Is there any chance you like monkeys?"

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:48.420
In 2014, a curious statistician worked through
the code of Netflix and found the site has

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:54.200
over 75,000 different "micro-genres" that
it uses to categorize movies and shows.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:58.250
These genres are largely hidden from the user,
and most people will rarely see even a fraction

00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:00.829
of them while browsing, but that's also kind
of the point.

00:03:00.829 --> 00:03:04.569
They'll tuck away those pics about werewolves
on wheels in place of family cartoons about

00:03:04.569 --> 00:03:06.829
unicorns if that seems to be your thing.

00:03:06.829 --> 00:03:10.200
And they can tell what you're really into
because ….

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.200
Tracking devices

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:15.739
One of the most valuable assets at Netflix's
disposal right now is its subscriber usage

00:03:15.739 --> 00:03:16.739
data.

00:03:16.739 --> 00:03:19.791
Along with collecting key information like
what you watch, when you watch it, and for

00:03:19.791 --> 00:03:24.629
how long, Netflix also pinpoints when you
pause, rewind, or stop watching something

00:03:24.629 --> 00:03:25.980
in an attempt to figure out why.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.519
"No, no , no, go past this, past this part.

00:03:28.519 --> 00:03:30.519
In fact, never play this again."

00:03:30.519 --> 00:03:33.310
This data on its own isn't all that useful.

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:36.819
But when cross-referenced with geographical
information and the same data produced by

00:03:36.819 --> 00:03:41.150
its millions of fellow subscribers, it can
be used to produce comprehensive charts detailing

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:44.930
exactly what people watch … and what they
give up on halfway through.

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:48.239
Netflix guards these details under lock and
key, but it does help them figure out what

00:03:48.239 --> 00:03:52.069
belongs in the permanent library collection
and what can be purged after a few months.

00:03:52.069 --> 00:03:56.230
So remember kids, the next time you press
pause, you may just be holding the fate of

00:03:56.230 --> 00:03:59.230
a feature's future in your hands.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:00.540
Original content

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:04.689
Thanks to its ad-free existence, Netflix doesn't
play the scripted content game quite like

00:04:04.689 --> 00:04:06.269
the television networks do.

00:04:06.269 --> 00:04:10.190
Their revenue stream comes from subscribers,
so writers of its original shows and movies

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:14.590
have a greater degree of flexibility when
it comes to length and narrative arc structures.

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:17.920
Whereas a show episode on cable might have
to factor in commercial breaks and chop up

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:21.540
the storyline accordingly, Netflix shows run
uninterrupted.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:25.420
And not only do showrunners have more creative
control as a result, but Netflix's subscriber

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:29.530
data helps them to know in advance which series
have the best chances of succeeding.

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:33.410
For example, with House of Cards, Netflix
had no qualms about giving director David

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:37.780
Fincher approximately $4 million per episode
because they had data to prove that users

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:39.840
loved the original British series.

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:43.851
And since their shows don't necessarily live
and die by their viewership ratings, Netflix

00:04:43.851 --> 00:04:48.060
alone gets to make the call whether to continue
that prestige series that's getting them on

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:52.410
the Emmys map but might not draw every eye
in the nation all at once.

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:53.620
Piracy prevention

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:57.841
In addition to tracking which shows its customers
watch and how often they press pause, Netflix

00:04:57.841 --> 00:05:01.530
also has analysts track what stingy people
are torrenting on the web.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:05.670
Like the data it collects from subscribers,
piracy figures represent an invaluable insight

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:09.610
into viewing habits that Netflix uses to adjust
its own programming.

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:13.100
After all, if people are willing to risk infecting
their computer with a virus to watch Game

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:17.060
of Thrones, it probably means someone is willing
to pay a few dollars to watch a similar show

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:19.090
from the comfort of their toilet.

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:22.940
Netflix has even been reported to use the
geographical data of pirates to adjust its

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:27.030
pricing, lowering the cost of their service
in areas with high rates of piracy in an attempt

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:29.140
to lure viewers over to the SS Netflix.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:32.800
It might not be enough to bring ashore every
swashbuckler on the web...

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:35.190
"A great pirate does not require such intricacy."

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:37.160
But it's certainly some solid reasoning.

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:38.160
Thanks for watching!

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:40.340
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:42.380
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

